---
title: Sobel sequences are impossible under standard [+]-> semantics
description: |
  A Sobel sequence requires: do(A) [+]-> q  and  do(A∪{b}) [+]-> ~q.

  Why this is impossible under Def 3.4:

  Def 3.4 (monotonicity of opposing): if α |> β and β ⊆ γ then α |> γ.
  This entails free_do(A∪{b}) ⊆ free_do(A) for all A, b — the witness set
  can only shrink as agents are added. If every free_do(A) history gives q,
  every free_do(A∪{b}) history does too, so do(A∪{b}) [+]-> ~q cannot hold.

  Note on antecedent strengthening: the two conditionals do not stand in a
  simple A→B vs A∧C→B relation, because the opposing term also changes:

    do(sd1) [+]-> q       = ∀h: (do(sd1,h) ∧ ¬opp(sd1,h))       → Xq(h)
    do({sd1,ha2}) [+]-> q = ∀h: (do(sd1,h) ∧ do(ha2,h) ∧ ¬opp({sd1,ha2},h)) → Xq(h)

  The deduction-theorem route to a monotonicity violation therefore does not
  apply. The impossibility follows from the set-inclusion argument above.

  This model verifies the containment computationally:
    free_do(sd1)      = {h1,h3,h5,h7}   (rb3 absent) — all give q
    free_do({sd1,ha2})= {h1}             (ha2 acts, rb3 absent, wn4 absent) — gives q
  The witness set shrinks as expected; both conditionals are True.

  For a concrete Sobel sequence under relaxed (non-monotone) opposing, see sobel.mmd.
actions:
  1: [sd, nop]
  2: [ha, nop]
  3: [rb, nop]
  4: [wn, nop]
opposings:
  sd1: [rb3]
  "{sd1,ha2}": [wn4]
evaluate:
  - - m/h1
    - "do(sd1) [+]-> q"
  - - m/h1
    - "do({1:sd, 2:ha}) [+]-> q"
---
